./.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260720-21bsr2_compiled-build-interop/README.md:10:The same fixture and expected outcomes run in both implementations; both reject the same negative cases and launch the same built command behavior; documentation includes a complete schema v6 example, toolchain prerequisites, cache behavior, security limits, and the process for adding future drivers; no release pins are changed before real commits/releases exist.
./.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260720-21bsr2_compiled-build-interop/TASK-260720-38l1sy_audit-curator-released-suite-pin/README.md:4:Verify that the Curator implementation story advances its protocol-suite pin only to the qualified released schema v6 revision and that the compiled-build consumer is a required cross-platform gate. Apply only narrowly scoped CI corrections if the handoff diverges.
./.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260720-21bsr2_compiled-build-interop/TASK-260720-38l1sy_audit-curator-released-suite-pin/README.md:7:Work in Curator after TASK-260720-25d05o and the Curator cross-platform CI task. Audit .github/workflows/ci.yml, every curator-spec checkout ref, the compiled-build conformance invocation, and any directly related no-skip guard. Candidate-phase testing may use an explicitly supplied CURATOR_CONFORMANCE_ROOT but must leave the committed release pin at the previous released suite. The eventual pin must equal the exact immutable protocol release commit recorded by TASK-260720-25d05o. Do not use a branch, mutable tag, feature-worktree SHA, guessed future release, or csk implementation detail.
./.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260720-21bsr2_compiled-build-interop/TASK-260720-38l1sy_audit-curator-released-suite-pin/README.md:10:Every Curator CI job that supplies CURATOR_CONFORMANCE_ROOT resolves the same qualified full curator-spec release commit and pin history shows that it moved only after TASK-260720-25d05o evidence existed. Linux, macOS, and Windows install the supported Go toolchain and execute the full Go suite consumer with no compiled-build case skipped, duplicated, or silently filtered. The focused interop job and go test ./... pass against the released suite, existing schema 1 through 5 coverage remains active, and a negative CI guard detects an old suite that lacks compiled-build cases. Any correction is limited to CI or test-gate wiring and the outcome records exact refs and CI links without fabricating a claim.
./.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260720-21bsr2_compiled-build-interop/TASK-260720-31zeo2_csk-shared-build-suite-consumer/README.md:7:Work in the csk repository after TASK-260720-12r55p, TASK-260720-3pemm6, and TASK-260720-2g7avf. Own the compiled-build additions to tests/test_protocol_conformance.py and narrowly related test-only helpers, using CURATOR_CONFORMANCE_ROOT. Exercise real manifest parsing, install planning, build and cache behavior, rollback and repair, shim launch, argument and exit propagation, and rejection ordering in isolated temporary homes and projects. Implement the suite-defined test-only result sink and record contract without adding a user-facing csk command. Extend the existing rc.4 vector consumer rather than replacing it. Do not call or import Curator, copy shared vectors into the repository, weaken no-skip behavior, or change the curator-spec CI pin in this task.
./.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260720-21bsr2_compiled-build-interop/TASK-260720-31zeo2_csk-shared-build-suite-consumer/README.md:10:With CURATOR_CONFORMANCE_ROOT set, the Python consumer parametrizes every shared compiled-build case exactly once and fails on unknown, missing, duplicate, xfailed, or skipped case IDs. When the suite-defined result sink is supplied, it emits exactly one schema-valid JSON Lines record per case with the verified suite digest, normalized outcome, compiler boundary, persistent-mutation class, and exact launch argv, stdout, stderr, and exit status; it emits no implementation-owned expected value or release claim. The unmodified shared fixture proves cache miss, verified hit, corrupt or untrusted rebuild, compiler-free and mutation-free dry-run including audit-registry state, build-root context and runtime exclusion, failed-build rollback, recovery, currentness, repair, locked GC, two-project isolation, and explicit launch. Every shared negative case is rejected in the expected normalized class and at the required before-build or before-mutation boundary. Test homes, project roots, Go caches, and subprocess recording are isolated per case; no binary hash or local oracle is introduced. Focused external-suite tests, the full pytest suite, and strict type checking pass when the suite is supplied, while developer runs may skip only when CURATOR_CONFORMANCE_ROOT and the result sink are both absent.
./.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260720-21bsr2_compiled-build-interop/TASK-260720-p7sdhg_language-driver-admission-matrix/README.md:7:Work in curator-spec after the compile-only decision and TASK-260720-3lo9jc. Own docs/build-driver-admission.md plus its README and decision-record navigation links. Classify Go, Rust and Cargo, Zig, Swift and SwiftPM, direct C and C++ compilers, Make, CMake, Meson, Java and Kotlin build paths, .NET and MSBuild, Node and TypeScript compilers and bundlers, Deno, and Python packaging. Do not reserve speculative driver identifiers, imply that offline equals safe, or weaken the closed go-v1 contract.
./.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260720-21bsr2_compiled-build-interop/TASK-260720-2g7avf_shared-compiled-build-interop-cases/README.md:4:Extend curator-spec with a manager-neutral executable interoperability layer that reuses the protocol-owned schema v6 Go fixture and normative build and lifecycle vectors. The shared suite is the only owner of inputs and expected outcomes, so Curator and csk remain independent consumers rather than copies of one another.
./.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260720-21bsr2_compiled-build-interop/TASK-260720-2g7avf_shared-compiled-build-interop-cases/README.md:10:The shared case file has stable case IDs and records the fixture path, operation, expected normalized outcome, expected persistent-mutation class, expected compiler-process class, and launch stdout, stderr, argument, and exit behavior where applicable. The suite-owned test contract names one caller-supplied result sink, a deterministic JSON Lines encoding, and required case_id, implementation, suite_sha256, outcome_class, compiler_process_class, persistent_mutation_class, launch_argv, launch_stdout, launch_stderr, and launch_exit_code fields without encoding release claims. The complete schema v6 fixture contains one build_roots entry, one go-v1 main command, a vendored dependency, a transitive embedded input, unrelated prompt context, and deterministic argument forwarding plus a nonzero-exit mode. Stable cases cover cache miss, verified hit, corrupt or untrusted rebuild, compiler-free dry-run, build-root context and runtime exclusion, explicit launch, failed-build rollback, interrupted recovery, currentness, repair, locked GC, and two-project isolation by reference rather than copied vector payloads. Every build-driver and compiled manager-lifecycle rejection case is included or referenced by stable ID with the same normalized class and before-build or before-mutation boundary. The generator imports no manager code, regeneration is byte-stable, legacy fixture values remain unchanged, and make validate plus make regenerate-check pass.
